    leadthreshold:{ sci:"Robert Kehoe (1893-1992)", topic:"The industry threshold for lead", lede:"Robert Kehoe brought the industry threshold for lead from the laboratory into pipes, taps, and public health.", no:13, profile:"The public-health water note today examines Robert Kehoe through the industry threshold for lead. Robert Kehoe conducted influential industry-funded lead research and promoted the idea that exposure was acceptable below a practical threshold. His framework dominated policy for decades despite limited evidence for a harmless level. A drinking-water system extends from source and treatment to miles of pipe and individual taps. Kehoe’s work shows why quality must be evaluated across that entire route.\n\nThe operational task is to scrutinize conflicts, comparison groups, detection limits, and whether absence of proof is being treated as proof of safety. Sampling location, stagnation time, treatment chemistry, pipe material, detection limits, exclusions, and population exposure should be documented before an average is called reassuring.\n\nWater can leave a plant compliant and become hazardous in distribution. Source changes alter pH, alkalinity, disinfectant demand, and protective scales, while sampling choices determine whether the highest exposures are visible. Public-health surveillance can expose failures that routine monitoring misses.\n\nThe water-safety conclusion: a regulatory threshold can reflect institutional power as well as biology. Protection requires treatment decisions, distribution chemistry, household measurements, and health evidence to agree. Residents’ observations are not laboratory results, but they are valid signals that determine where testing should begin. Discarding high values without a documented technical reason changes the question instead of answering it. Treatment changes should trigger focused distribution sampling before protective pipe scales have time to destabilize widely. Public notices need plain explanations of who is most vulnerable and which immediate steps meaningfully reduce exposure.",
      frame:"Operator Nunez fills two sample bottles at The City Manager's Office. \"Same city, different taps, different histories. Explain the industry threshold for lead.\"", q:[
        { q:"Which water-quality interpretation best captures Robert Kehoe’s work on the industry threshold for lead?",
          o:[
            { t:"Robert Kehoe conducted influential industry-funded lead research and promoted the idea that exposure was acceptable below a practical threshold. Household exposure remains measurable. Distribution chemistry stays visible.", v:"expert", fb:"Correct: the answer connects source treatment, distribution chemistry, tap sampling, and health evidence." },
            { t:"Robert Kehoe contributed to the industry threshold for lead, but the account tests finished water at the plant without household taps, pipe materials, or corrosion conditions. Population evidence is incomplete.", v:"partial", fb:"This measurement is useful but cannot represent the full distribution system or exposed population." },
            { t:"The summary wrongly attributes to Robert Kehoe the claim that one regulatory average proves safety for every resident and building. Plant data cannot establish it. Plant measurements cannot describe every household tap.", v:"wrong", fb:"That conclusion mistakes plant compliance or a selected average for household safety." },
            { t:"Robert Kehoe is used to discard high samples and complaints because they complicate the official compliance calculation. Averages hide the highest exposure. Cost pressure postpones the needed corrosion control.", v:"danger", fb:"That response suppresses inconvenient signals instead of testing the preventable exposure pathway." }
           ] },
        { q:"Which investigation best tests exposure across the water system?",
          o:[
            { t:"Conduct this water-system investigation: scrutinize conflicts, comparison groups, detection limits, and whether absence of proof is being treated as proof of safety. Pipe-scale evidence stays beside the household samples.", v:"expert", fb:"Correct: the answer connects source treatment, distribution chemistry, tap sampling, and health evidence." },
            { t:"Measure source and plant chemistry carefully, but omit stagnation sampling, premise plumbing, and exposed children’s records. High-risk taps remain untested. Population exposure cannot be inferred from this location alone.", v:"partial", fb:"This measurement is useful but cannot represent the full distribution system or exposed population." },
            { t:"Infer that low lead at the intake rules out release from service lines and household plumbing downstream. The pipe chemistry contradicts that. Plant data cannot establish it. Plant measurements cannot describe every household tap.", v:"wrong", fb:"That conclusion mistakes plant compliance or a selected average for household safety." },
            { t:"Preserve the reassuring average by excluding high taps, delaying corrosion treatment, and calling symptoms unrelated. High samples are removed to protect the headline. Cost pressure postpones the needed corrosion control.", v:"danger", fb:"That response suppresses inconvenient signals instead of testing the preventable exposure pathway." }
           ] },
        { q:"Which public-health principle is most defensible?",
          o:[
            { t:"The public-health lesson is that a regulatory threshold can reflect institutional power as well as biology. Distribution chemistry stays visible.", v:"expert", fb:"Correct: the answer connects source treatment, distribution chemistry, tap sampling, and health evidence." },
            { t:"Plant compliance can stand in for distribution-system safety if disinfection and turbidity remain within limits. High-risk taps remain untested.", v:"partial", fb:"This measurement is useful but cannot represent the full distribution system or exposed population." },
            { t:"Clear water without unusual taste or odor provides practical evidence that toxic metal exposure is absent. Plant data cannot establish it.", v:"wrong", fb:"That conclusion mistakes plant compliance or a selected average for household safety." },
            { t:"The problem must be a rogue operator or needless panic, excluding a source change and suppressed warning data. Averages hide the highest exposure.", v:"danger", fb:"That response suppresses inconvenient signals instead of testing the preventable exposure pathway." }
           ] }
      ] },
