  story:[ "<p>Rushton residents are collecting bottled water while city reports continue to describe the tap supply as compliant.</p>", "<p><b>Operator Nunez</b> operated treatment, <b>The Water Chemist</b> measured the samples, and <b>The Clerk</b> filed the complaints and directives.</p>", "<p><b>A rogue operator poisoning the supply</b> and <b>Nothing wrong — the water meets the rules</b> turn a distribution-system question into either one bad actor or no problem at all.</p>", "<p>A public-health decision is due in eight days, before another round of official sampling begins.</p>" ],
  endings:{ overclaimWhat:"tampering", dismissalWhat:"safe",
    win:{ expertTitle:"The Pipes Enter the Finding", expert:["You name <b>Merrick — the city's emergency manager</b>, locate the suppressed correspondence in <b>The City Manager's Office</b>, and prove <b>Corrosion control skipped and the warnings suppressed</b>. Neither a rogue operator poisoning the supply nor nothing wrong — the water meets the rules explains the tap and blood data.","The source switch changed corrosion chemistry, high household samples were excluded, and children’s exposure rose afterward. The evidence connects an administrative decision to a distribution-system hazard."], soundTitle:"The Exposure Map", sound:["Your water-system finding correctly links <b>Merrick — the city's emergency manager</b>, <b>Corrosion control skipped and the warnings suppressed</b>, and the city records in <b>The City Manager's Office</b>.","House-to-house variation remains, yet the missing treatment and selective reporting are sufficient for immediate public-health action."], namedTitle:"The Corrosion Decision", named:["You select the correct water inquiry combination: <b>Merrick — the city's emergency manager</b>, <b>The City Manager's Office</b>, and <b>Corrosion control skipped and the warnings suppressed</b>.","The account is short, but it points to source chemistry, tap sampling, resident complaints, and the omitted corrosion safeguard."] },
    overclaim:{ title:"The Operator Was Not Poisoning Water", body:["You accuse <b>A rogue operator poisoning the supply</b>, turning a systemwide chemical change into the secret act of one plant worker.","When intent cannot be shown, the city can dismiss the entire allegation and avoid the documented treatment and reporting decisions."] },
    dismissal:{ title:"Compliance Was Not Safety", body:["You endorse <b>Nothing wrong — the water meets the rules</b>, allowing selected compliance statistics to outrank actual household exposure.","That conclusion leaves pipe-scale loss, high tap values, and elevated blood lead unexplained while residents continue drinking the same water."] },
    wrongNames:{ title:"Right Chemistry, Wrong Office", body:["You establish <b>Corrosion control skipped and the warnings suppressed</b>, but blame the wrong official or place its authorization in the laboratory. The city correspondence points to"] } },
